\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Flat cohomology is a finite Koszul calculation}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For a represented-flat connection, let #{H_i} be the
commuting seam holonomies of \ref{fdip-00SV}. The cochain
complex #{(C^*(X;F),\extd_U)} is homotopy equivalent over #{k}
to
##{\left(\Lambda^*(k^n)\otimes_k F,
\sum_i(e_i\wedge)(H_i^{-1}-\bbI)\right).}
If each order #{r_i} of #{H_i} is a unit in #{k}, then
##{H^p(C^*(X;F),\extd_U)
\cong\Lambda^p(k^n)\otimes_k F^{H_1,\ldots,H_n}.}
Without the unit-order hypothesis the first complex remains the
cohomology calculation; the displayed simplification is not asserted.}

\proof{
\p{First consider one coordinate with period #{N} and seam
#{H}, and put #{A=H^{-1}-\bbI}. The differential on #{F^N}
has values #{f(r+1)-f(r)} for #{r<N-1}, and
#{H^{-1}f(0)-f(N-1)} at the seam. Define maps to and from
#{F\xrightarrow{A}F} by
##{\begin{aligned}
\pi_0f&=f(0),&\quad j_0v&=(v,\ldots,v),\\
\pi_1a&=\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}a(r),& j_1w&=(0,\ldots,0,w).
\end{aligned}}
Set #{h(a)(0)=0} and #{h(a)(r)=\sum_{t<r}a(t)}. Telescoping
gives #{\pi_1\extd=A\pi_0}, #{\extd j_0=j_1A},
#{\pi j=\bbI}, and
##{h\extd=\bbI-j_0\pi_0,\qquad
\extd h=\bbI-j_1\pi_1.}
For the second identity, every non-seam component is #{a(r)};
at the seam it is #{-\sum_{t<N-1}a(t)}. For #{N=1}, #{h=0}
and both comparison maps are identities.}

\p{Apply these maps one coordinate at a time after
\ref{fdip-00SV}. All other seam operators commute with the
maps because the #{H_i} commute. On exterior degree, give
#{h} the sign of contraction in that coordinate. Its
anticommutator with each other-direction differential is zero:
the coefficient maps commute and insertion past contraction
has the opposite sign. The displayed one-dimensional homotopy
identity therefore holds on the full total complex at each
step. Successive steps give the stated Koszul complex without
division.}

\p{For its split calculation set
##{P_i=\frac1{r_i}\sum_{a=0}^{r_i-1}H_i^{-a},\qquad
Q_i=\frac1{r_i}\sum_{a=0}^{r_i-1}aH_i^{-a}.}
The finite sums give #{P_i^2=P_i},
#{\imP_i=\ker(H_i-\bbI)}, and
##{(H_i^{-1}-\bbI)Q_i=Q_i(H_i^{-1}-\bbI)=\bbI-P_i.}
For the last equality, shifting the sum leaves #{r_i\bbI}
minus the geometric sum. All these maps commute. The identity
##{\bbI=P_1\cdots P_n+
\sum_{i=1}^n P_1\cdots P_{i-1}(\bbI-P_i)}
is a decomposition into mutually orthogonal idempotents.
On the image of the #{i}-th summand, #{Q_i(e_i\cdot)} is a
contracting homotopy for the Koszul differential: their
anticommutator is #{\bbI-P_i=\bbI}, and cross terms cancel.
On the common fixed image, the differential is zero. This
proves the cohomology formula. It does not assert that the
fixed module or a ring cohomology module is free.}
}
